update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('a9e13024-e182-4076-8269-c29edba9d2ed'::uuid,'45ffc3ac4080fe64e8bd2d50ad191a98','B matches the first sentence once we drop the opener. "Contrary to recent speculations" only tells us whose view the author is rejecting; the claim itself is that no hardware store will be opening in the plaza. B says exactly that.

Run the support test: why believe B? Because anyone opening a store would already be publicizing it, and nobody is. Both premises answer the question, so B is the main point. When a conclusion arrives wrapped in a phrase that names the view being rejected, match the claim, not the wrapper.'),
('b4f6aa47-cfcf-4be5-86c5-374667eadeff'::uuid,'ff66d39a72c4c8d1319b8e4829cd4bf0','The author never says whether opening a hardware store there would be wise. The argument is a prediction about what will happen, based on the lack of publicity, not a judgment about whether it is a good idea. A Main Conclusion answer has to restate a claim the author actually made, so D is out.'),
('e856c25c-f61c-4369-bc96-934b14f2805d'::uuid,'6a079ca1b793e36994b03c2515c2be9e','A restates the first sentence, the "given." Ask what it does in the argument: nothing supports it, and it is not offered as a reason for anything. It explains why inspiring curiosity matters. Background is not the main conclusion, so A is out.

Intriguing → Inspires others'' curiosity'),
('cb29a944-8e8a-4483-b333-34ab24c9aaf3'::uuid,'c7e304e11fd2f7868f17b5dab652963d','B is the middle sentence in conditional form. Broadening your abilities and extending your intellectual reach is the trigger; being able to inspire the perpetual curiosity of others is the result.

Expands mind → Inspires others'' curiosity

Run the support test. Why believe B? Because constant expansion makes you impossible to fully comprehend, and so a constant mystery. The "For" sentence answers that question, so B is what the argument is built to prove. When a conclusion is a conditional, match the direction as well as the words.'),
('7960b72b-6e77-4692-9642-59619f7abc3d'::uuid,'eed3c6893096bf4d9e428ac6ca517f09','C restates part of the last sentence, the one introduced by "For." That sentence is the reason for the middle claim, so C is support, not the main point. C also drops the idea of expanding one''s mind, which is the trigger the conclusion is about.

Not fully comprehended → Constant mystery'),
('02391206-401d-4b1c-9c7b-de7462a9c9ee'::uuid,'9a28ab4c03556c4c84e820ca5ae373ed','D reverses the conclusion. The author says expanding your mind is enough to inspire curiosity. D says it is required: to inspire curiosity, one must constantly broaden.

Argument: Expands mind → Inspires others'' curiosity
D: Inspires others'' curiosity → Expands mind

Could someone inspire perpetual curiosity some other way? The argument never rules it out. D is tempting because it uses all the right words, just in the wrong direction.'),
('99213936-aad6-40ec-b9c2-28d56acfeccb'::uuid,'8d30952d4458d88a3624625c06d00211','C restates the start of the manager''s first reason. Ask why we should believe the opening claim, and the fact that many jobs require little or no creativity is part of the answer. That puts C below the conclusion as support, not at the top as the main point.'),
('4da7a1cb-8a7d-4d8f-96ec-ec6cbce71d48'::uuid,'22f65d06cc37d20141f24a83bcd6c81a','D matches the first sentence. The phrase "a proper goal of an employee training program" becomes "employee training programs should promote creativity," and D keeps the manager''s hedge, "no good reason to suppose." Run the support test: why believe D? Because many jobs don''t need creativity, and creativity may not be teachable anyway. Both reasons support D, and D supports nothing else. That is how you confirm a main conclusion.'),
('e00544fe-c9b3-49e1-92d0-1bc34d06b6fb'::uuid,'507c7eb9321b83434b5e0fec3da110c3','A restates the anthropologist''s reason, the claim after "because": humans did not evolve from chimpanzees. Its "some extinct species" reflects the common-ancestor point. Ask why the author thinks it does not follow that chimps have language, and A is the answer. That makes A evidence, not the main conclusion.'),
('527489d4-3984-4b3b-b8a1-138eb0b59331'::uuid,'fb7a431c35f85625eb2b6823ca70c253','B is the conclusion in abstract form. "The assumption that something like human language must exist in some species from which humans evolved" is the reasoning the author grants. And "has no clearcut linguistic implications for chimpanzees" restates "it simply does not follow that chimpanzees have anything like human language."

B also keeps the limit: it does not say chimps lack language, only that this reasoning tells us nothing clear about them. When the right answer is abstract, translate each phrase back into the stimulus and check that the pieces line up.'),
('0cde3694-2ad7-4343-bc7c-ddebd59c30eb'::uuid,'d6e6e05ae4fa2b385ac5315c3504e7a7','C says chimpanzee communication is cruder than human language. That idea comes from the opposing reasoning ("albeit cruder"), which the anthropologist refuses to apply to chimps. The author''s point is that we cannot conclude chimps have anything like human language at all, so the author never asserts C, let alone concludes it.'),
('8a58237e-e393-40fd-adfa-7115846762d7'::uuid,'83a1fa62eedd30c8133828b1e48c791d','Intelligence never comes up. The anthropologist explains the gap with family history: humans and chimpanzees share a common ancestor, and language may have started after it died out. D offers a different explanation, and it says chimpanzees lack something, which goes further than the author''s claim that their having language does not follow.'),
('7666980f-7c18-42f6-8c30-dadb4ef8cf00'::uuid,'4c1cc971dbee00d6e9ce18b2ee512cac','E takes the last sentence and makes it stronger. The anthropologist says human language "might easily have begun" after the common ancestor died out. E says it did begin then. The author only needs the possibility to block the inference to chimpanzees, and even the softer version is a reason for the conclusion, not the conclusion.'),
('908666f1-4551-407c-af0f-6381ce29a83f'::uuid,'0a4149e83b27e15cc65cc1c6ec3be5d4','A is a claim the pundit never makes. The argument says new governments tend to reform early, not that the threat of losing office makes new leaders more motivated. A Main Conclusion answer restates a claim from the paragraph, and the conclusion here is the first sentence. A predicts a new effect instead.'),
('f827d005-b785-4af6-a7aa-2ce1676658a3'::uuid,'4ff263c7db7f6186c855488e2c667f02','B blends pieces of the evidence into a new claim. The pundit says leaders who wait must deny the problem, deny anything could be done, or admit responsibility. That is a list of options, not a claim that long-serving leaders "tend to deny responsibility." And even a fair version would be support for the first sentence, not the point it supports.'),
('47d7192f-d3dd-4315-879e-61a7285b5ff5'::uuid,'2152bdce4713f4f0ea002bf236737de9','C restates the second sentence: reforms are generally undertaken early in a new government. The pundit introduces it with "The reason is that," which marks it as support for the first sentence. Ask why it is good to vote leaders out, and C is part of the answer, so it is not the conclusion.'),
('95595b1a-4b0c-4448-b045-36b7bc4f7af7'::uuid,'4ff263c7db7f6186c855488e2c667f02','D adds a recommendation the pundit never makes. It says leaders who act right away should get enough time to succeed, but the pundit''s claim is that it is good to vote leaders out after a few years. The pundit never discusses how much time a leader needs to solve a problem, so D cannot be the claim the argument supports.'),
('052f0830-bce6-45d4-9ede-6e31e25f3839'::uuid,'11f59de32e465db9bda80e8996876f3b','E matches the first sentence. "It is good to have national leaders voted out of office after a few years" becomes "National leaders should be removed from office every few years by the voting in of new leaders." Saying a practice is good and saying it should be done are close enough for Main Conclusion.

Run the support test: why should leaders be voted out? Because reforms come early, and leaders who wait get stuck denying problems or owning them. The rest of the paragraph supports E, and E supports nothing else.'),
('7a4bf07f-b86a-4024-a782-170178176a65'::uuid,'cd705559f4cc7c47a5bca4c1aae085a3','A restates the first sentence: many Internet users long for emotional connection to a global community. That is the setup the columnist reacts to after "However." The columnist never argues for it; it just describes people. A claim that nothing supports and that the argument builds on is background, not the conclusion.'),
('6b71dbac-80d4-490c-ae74-d4fd82d3f164'::uuid,'41199856b62cc577dadad35f3f50af97','B restates the second sentence: the longing leads people to use the Internet to learn about other cultures. Like A, it is setup. The conclusion is the columnist''s verdict on this behavior, that it probably won''t satisfy the longing. B describes the behavior without the verdict.'),
('dc75a1f3-19ed-417e-97c2-35d890fa78aa'::uuid,'5f4107065e41619f40fbb435e7e92cc2','C paraphrases the reason, the clause after "for": the Internet is used primarily for acquiring information rather than developing feelings of interconnectedness. "for" marks support. Ask why the longing won''t be satisfied, and C is the answer, so it is evidence, not the main point.'),
('072c08b5-64cd-4817-8c60-cb02a4261d62'::uuid,'5a73fb3becadc554a99c415c468e0c38','D matches the sentence after "However." Its "learning about other cultures through the Internet probably will not provide emotional connection" is the columnist''s "probably will not satisfy their longing." D keeps the hedge, keeps the Internet setting, and names the right people: those desiring connection to a global human community.

A useful check on Main Conclusion: when a pivot word like "However" is followed later by a support word like "for," the conclusion often sits between them.'),
('722eee11-4785-4f6f-a706-d61d1b78535b'::uuid,'32993111d898b7bb283fe9fbddbd811c','E widens the claim. The columnist talks only about using the Internet, and the reason is tied to the Internet: it is mainly for information. E covers any "behavior that results in the acquisition of knowledge about other cultures," which would include travel or reading. E also drops "probably" and says such behavior "rarely produces" connection, a general claim the columnist never makes.'),
('784154d3-91a9-4bff-9bc3-c9ffede00744'::uuid,'c73eaf6a75071a3c8c6d5c8b5faa8c94','A restates the visionaries'' forecast from the first sentence. The author brings up that view only to push back with "But." Nothing in the paragraph argues for A, and the author''s own claim is about who benefits from home banking. A is the opposing view, not the conclusion.'),
('55857aef-7f50-4226-8bb5-ec695429f11a'::uuid,'990c1025760e26b80a8480358dbfae3f','B matches the sentence after "But." The word "that" refers back to transacting all financial business from home, and "more of a paradise for banks than for their customers" becomes "more beneficial to banks than to their customers." B keeps both the comparison and "may."

When a conclusion uses a word like "that" to point back, work out what it refers to before you match. The right answer will usually spell it out, as B does.'),
('818466bb-800f-44b8-abd9-e79e53f912c4'::uuid,'55fab6b90ce26b4893c7455880cd2f6a','C restates the last sentence: as banks cut branches and staff, customers serve as their own tellers and pay more fees. Ask why home banking may favor banks over customers, and C is the answer. That makes it evidence for the conclusion, not the conclusion itself.'),
('fde78b54-6b5d-4261-bd67-4c9080c33ede'::uuid,'eab84ccbece81f92bf2d1266e040fb39','D says only that cutting branches and staff would benefit banks. The author''s claim is a comparison: home banking may be better for banks than for customers. D drops the customers and the comparison, and it shifts the subject from home banking to the cuts that come with it.'),
('bccd5dc9-a22c-4ba7-8cbc-1e6d250e4ed9'::uuid,'3112006946becc6bbb4276d3522b003d','E links home banking to banks eliminating branches and staff. The last sentence assumes that link, but the author never argues for it. More importantly, the conclusion is about who benefits more from home banking, and E says nothing about benefit at all.'),
('dadf5035-217f-4572-a398-46eefb908320'::uuid,'e210cd25beb7c5328063fc0d15c183b4','A takes the evidence about social movements and inflates it. The author says popular music "has the potential to contribute to" converting nonmembers and boosting morale. Contributing to a movement is not the same as accounting for its success. And even a modest version would be support for the first sentence, not the main point.'),
('d8f86a89-9307-4a91-b533-ae10c0fc5553'::uuid,'c8369e62c9afb6418e92b4d735c4b081','B is tempting because the first sentence mentions people who "simply enjoy" the music. But the author''s claim is that these listeners miss its other uses, not that they give entertainment too much weight. Not knowing about one use is different from overvaluing another, and nothing in the paragraph argues that entertainment value is overemphasized.'),
('20d77399-3d52-4fb3-a581-36048f2ce04e'::uuid,'1edec078d11fd2dc5a008a616e1b91d7','C turns a modest claim, that popular music can contribute to converting nonmembers, into "the most effective way" of converting people. The author never compares popular music with any other way of winning people over. And conversion sits in the deepest layer of support, so even a fair version of C would be evidence, not the conclusion.'),
('76b5fd66-7fcf-4f84-beff-8805c19eea8d'::uuid,'5b156af67aefcc3cdd1722e6056f87b3','D states the core of the first sentence. If popular music "has been used to express religious and political messages," then it has purposes other than mere entertainment, the "simply enjoy" use the first sentence sets those messages against.

D leaves out the claim that many listeners don''t realize this, but it keeps the part the evidence supports: social movements using music to spread their views shows it does more than entertain. On Main Conclusion, the right answer can be a more general restatement of the claim, as long as it says nothing the author didn''t.'),
('11e05fdf-74f6-4679-9730-914509d5450f'::uuid,'0ab71b839ed6fdefad665fbf053744cf','Emotional impact never comes up. Morale and solidarity might sound emotional, but they are listed as reasons movements adopt music, not claims about how music affects listeners in general. E''s "profound emotional impact on its listeners" is a new claim the author neither states nor supports, so it cannot be the main conclusion.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;